<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Émile Durkheim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as David Émile Durkheim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Émile Durkheim was a French sociologist who formally established the academic discipline of sociology. Born in Épinal into a family of devout Alsatian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, he taught the first social science course at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> from 1887. There he published The Division of Labour in Society (1893) and The Rules of Sociological Method (1895), set up the first European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of sociology, wrote Le Suicide (1897) and founded the journal L'Année sociologique in 1898. He moved to the Sorbonne in 1902 and published The Elementary Forms of the Religious Life in 1912. His son André died at the front in 1915, and Durkheim died after a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1917.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emile-durkheim.jpg" alt="Émile Durkheim" width="330" height="449" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Émile_Durkheim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Durkheim was the son of Mélanie and Moïse Durkheim and came from a line of rabbis stretching back eight generations, including his father, grandfather and great-grandfather. He began his education in a rabbinical school but switched schools at an early age and led a secular life, arguing in much of his work that religious phenomena arise from social rather than divine factors. He kept ties with his family and the Jewish community, and many of his collaborators and students were Jewish. Marcel Mauss was his nephew. He was influenced by the sociological positivism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-comte">Auguste Comte</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Durkheim worked on three goals: to establish sociology as a distinct academic discipline, to analyse how modern societies keep their integrity when shared religious and ethnic background can no longer be assumed, and to draw out the practical implications of scientific knowledge. In The Rules of Sociological Method (1895) he defined a social fact as a way of acting capable of exercising an external constraint on the individual, and held that observation must be as impartial as possible and that sociology should favour comparison.</p><p>He named anomie, a lack of social norms, and forced division of labour as the two chief pathologies of society. In The Elementary Forms of the Religious Life (1912) he defined religion as a unified system of beliefs and practices relative to sacred things, uniting all adherents in one moral community called a Church. He sought the social origin and function of religion and a common element across cultures, and he divided reality into the sacred and the profane rather than the natural and the supernatural. His analysis of morality, which he defined as a system of rules for conduct, drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/immanuel-kant">Immanuel Kant</a>'s notion of duty while criticising parts of Kant's theory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> affected him deeply. He worked to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the war, but his reluctance to accept simple nationalist fervour, combined with his Jewish background, made him a target of the French Right. Many of his former students were drafted and died in the trenches. His son André died at the front in December 1915, a loss from which he never recovered, and he suffered a stroke in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 15 November 1917 and died. He was buried at the Montparnasse Cemetery. Marcel Mauss, Maurice Halbwachs, Alfred Radcliffe-Brown, Talcott Parsons, Claude Lévi-Strauss and Pierre Bourdieu are among those influenced by him, and the establishment of sociology as a recognised discipline is among his lasting legacies.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Émile Durkheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
